mode opt

// `mode opt` holds this whole file — and every module it imports — to witchy's
// fast-path discipline. Two things change versus an ordinary file:
//
//   1. A heap-typed parameter (List, String, Dict, record, …) must declare an
//      ownership convention: `let` (immutable borrow, no copy), `var` (mutate and
//      write back), or `own` (take ownership). Scalars and capabilities are exempt.
//   2. Any accumulation that would silently fall back to O(n²) copying becomes a
//      compile error instead of a quiet slowdown.
//
// `mode opt` is also TRANSITIVE: an `opt` module may only import other `opt`
// modules (the bundled standard library is exempt), so the guarantee covers the
// whole reachable graph, not just this one file. See examples/projects/opt_pipeline
// for a multi-file version, and rfcs/performance-modes.md for the full story.
fn main(console: Console):
    // Building a list by self-assign push stays O(n): the uniqueness analysis
    // proves `xs` is unaliased, so each push mutates in place (capacity doubling)
    // rather than copying the whole list. In an ordinary file a shape that fell
    // off this path would just be a note; under `mode opt` it would be an error.
    var xs = []
    for i in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]:
        xs = list.push(xs, i * i)
    print(console, "squares: ${xs}")
    print(console, "sum:     ${sum(xs)}")

// `let xs` borrows the list immutably — the type checker proves `sum` never lets
// it escape, so the caller passes a reference with no defensive copy. Drop the
// `let` here and `mode opt` rejects the file: heap params must be explicit.
pub fn sum(let xs: List(Int)) -> Int:
    var acc = 0
    for x in xs:
        acc = acc + x
    acc
